   130	- **P3 — Key binding (anti-substitution).** Acceptance binds each
   131	  signature to the issuer identity committed in the signed bytes. The
   132	  adversary model (A1.3) explicitly includes key-substitution attacks
   133	  (DSKS / exclusive-ownership failures: constructing a different keypair
   134	  under which an existing message–signature pair verifies, then
   135	  re-presenting the package under a substituted manifest). Idealized
   136	  signatures assume this binding by fiat; Ed25519 signature verification
   137	  alone does not provide protocol-level issuer binding or exclusive
   138	  ownership — the verification equation is not, by itself, the
   139	  issuer-binding protocol. Discharge is split:
   140	  - the binding of identity into signed bytes — the signed bytes include,
   141	    at minimum: domain-separation tag, object type (per P7's enumeration),
   142	    algorithm identifier, issuer identity, key fingerprint, manifest hash,
   143	    and canonicalization version — is **[model]**;
   144	  - the implementation's **verification profile** is **[assumption]**,
   145	    named in Layer 2 and discharged as H1a evidence: exact library and
   146	    version, accepted signature encodings, public-key validation behavior,
   147	    low-order-point handling, cofactor equation variant, and negative test
   148	    vectors for each, all recorded in the repository.
